  "query_preview": "..Mostly clear skies across southeast Arizona this \nmorning as the area is under the influence of strong high pressure \naloft over New Mexico. Latest GOES total precipitable water shows \nquite a bit of drying with PWAT values down to 0.75\" to 1 inch from \nTucson eastward, though locales west of Tucson are hanging onto a \nhigher moisture levels with PWATs of 1.25\" to 1.5\" over the T.O. \nNation and western deserts. Given the drier conditions for much of \nthe area and subsidence from the high aloft, we're expecting another \ndown day for storm activity, though we can't rule out a rogue weak \nstorm or two across Santa Cruz or south central Pima County where \ninstability and moisture levels are better aligned. \n\nThe high pressure aloft on Wednesday nudges west along the AZ/NM \nborder with another day of very limited storm activity expected \ngiven the subsidence from the high and continued overall lack of \nmoisture. Once again, Santa Cruz County into south central Pima \nCount would be the favored region for any storms.\n\nFor Thursday, not much movement on the high near the AZ/NM border. \nMoisture levels start ticking higher to support 10 to 30 percent \nstorm chances from Tucson south and eastward, though the highest \nchances will be along the Int'l border and mountains. \n\nA transition back to a more typical monsoon pattern will occur \nFriday, continuing this weekend into early next week as the high \naloft moves east, putting southeast Arizona in a more favorable \nregion for daily monsoonal activity with east to southeast flow as \nPWATs increase to above normal levels this weekend. Daily storm \nchances during this period will typically be in the 20 to 60 percent \nrange with the usual impacts including gusty winds and areas of \nflash flooding. \n\nThe Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect through Saturday with \nhighs 6 to 10 degrees above normal and the heat peaking today \nthrough Friday.",
